resources.string:
Android has no kernel driver for CH340/CH341, and none is needed: this tool ships drivers for the common USB-to-serial chips (CH340, CH341, CP2102, FT232/FT2232, PL2303, standard CDC).

If no device is found, check in order:
1. the phone supports and enables OTG (some models need USB permission in developer options);
2. the adapter is powered — the status bar should show "USB device detected";
3. the chip VID/PID is not in the built-in driver list — use a CH340/CP2102 adapter instead;
4. "Deny" was tapped in the system dialog — unplug, reconnect and allow it.

Purpose: download .bin/.hex firmware into the STM32 internal flash over a USB-to-serial adapter (CH340 etc.), using the serial ISP protocol of the bootloader burned into the chip (8 data bits / even parity / 1 stop bit).

Wiring: cross TX/RX between the adapter and the chip UART, GND must be connected. To enter the bootloader automatically you also need DTR→BOOT0 and RTS→NRST (many breakout adapters expose these pins).

Steps:
1. Plug in the adapter → rescan → connect;
2. choose the bootloader entry method (default 1: RTS reset + DTR for BOOT0; pick 0 manual if those two lines are not wired);
3. select firmware: .hex carries its own addresses, .bin needs the "BIN start address" field (usually 08000000);
4. tap "Start download". The log shows sync, erase, write and verify step by step.

Note: downloading performs a full chip erase, so the program already in the chip is removed — use "Read back flash" in the menu first if you want a backup. Removing read protection also erases everything.
